  "episodes": [
    {
      "slug": "polaris-fellowship",
      "index": "01",
      "title": "The Polaris Signal.",
      "subtitle": "Shortlisted for Round 4 of India's most selective student fellowship.",
      "era": "S01 · 2026 : 2026",
      "date": "JUL 2026",
      "category": "Recognition",
      "excerpt": "Out of thousands of applicants nationwide, shortlisted for Round 4, the final interview round of the Polaris Fellowship, for a shot at one of ten seats in Bangalore.",
      "aspect": "aspect-video",
      "accent": "primary",
      "image": "https://res.cloudinary.com/dj57qnyzd/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/portfolio/journey/polaris/Top-100-Polaris.png",
      "videos": [
        {
          "title": "Round 2 · Introduction Video",
          "youtubeId": "hrUCs4Wt5c4"
        },
        {
          "title": "Round 3 · Technical Deep-Dive",
          "youtubeId": "-jAFntReQLY"
        }
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Thousands of students, builders, and researchers applied to the Polaris Fellowship 2026: India's most selective residential program, ten seats nationwide, six months embedded inside the country's sharpest startups and venture firms. Round 1 filtered the noise. Round 2 asked for an unscripted video: who are you beyond the resume, what have you actually shipped, what's the most ambitious thing worth building in the next ten years."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 06 / Selection"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "Receipts, not roadmaps."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Round 3 changed the question: not what you plan to build, but what you've already built, in the trenches, where it broke and what no tutorial covered. The brief was to walk through one project in full technical depth: architecture, the hardest problem solved, and what would be done differently next time."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quote",
          "text": "genuine depth on something real"
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Clearing Round 3's technical deep dive earned a place in Round 4, the final round: a thirty to forty five minute interview with the Polaris panel, the last filter before ten seats are named in Bangalore."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "agentops-journey",
      "index": "02",
      "title": "The Agentic Economy.",
      "subtitle": "Building the policy firewall for autonomous agent payments on Casper.",
      "era": "S01 · 2026 : 2026",
      "date": "JUL 2026",
      "category": "Governance",
      "excerpt": "AI agents are about to hold real spending power. AgentOps is the control plane that lets them build without handing over a blank check, proven live and anchored on-chain on Casper.",
      "aspect": "aspect-video",
      "accent": "secondary",
      "videos": [
        {
          "title": "Casper Hackathon Build",
          "youtubeId": "xy9PJHcfa8I"
        }
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Autonomous agents are being handed real spending power before anyone builds them a conscience. An agent with a private key and no spend cap, no velocity limit, no kill-switch, and no tamper-proof record of what it did with the money it was given is unbounded risk. Today, the only insight into a misbehaving agent is a bank statement after the damage is done."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 07 / Governance"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "A policy firewall, not a wallet."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "AgentOps sits between the agent and the chain. Every payment, trade, and deploy an agent attempts is checked against operator-defined rules in real time: spend caps, velocity limits, destination scope, signed or denied on the spot, with a budget hold placed atomically so an agent can never double-spend against an in-flight payment."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quote",
          "text": "Prove every decision forever on Casper, immutably and on-chain."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Once a decision settles, its hash is anchored to a custom Rust/Odra smart contract, the GuardRegistry, where re-anchoring the same hash is a no-op and submitting a different hash for an already-anchored decision causes the contract to revert. The audit trail cannot be rewritten later, not even by the team that built it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "veil-journey",
      "index": "03",
      "title": "Privacy as a Habit.",
      "subtitle": "Extending Tesseract's thesis into a full private wallet on Stellar.",
      "era": "S01 · 2026 : 2026",
      "date": "JUN 2026",
      "category": "Privacy",
      "excerpt": "From a single private-payment mechanism to a complete wallet: shielded notes, private sends, batch payouts, and private prediction markets, all on Stellar.",
      "aspect": "aspect-video",
      "accent": "primary",
      "videos": [
        {
          "title": "Stellar Zk Hacks Build",
          "youtubeId": "CIWCdmyTbRY"
        }
      ],
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Tesseract proved a mechanism: deposit, wait, withdraw, and the link between depositor and recipient disappears. It was one door. VEIL asks what happens when privacy needs to be the entire building: a wallet where every surface, not just the transfer, understands how to stay quiet."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 08 / Continuity"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "One note model, every surface."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Public USDC converts into shielded notes on deposit. Those notes spend privately, split into change, batch into payouts, or convert into Market Notes for private prediction-market positions, all through the same commitment model, so the private path is never a special case bolted onto the public one. A backend prover and relayer generate proofs, simulate transactions, and settle pool state on Stellar testnet."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quote",
          "text": "Privacy should be part of the wallet flow itself."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "What shipped is a live client with wallet and market privacy pools, an encrypted client-side vault, deposit and withdrawal flows, note transfers, batch payments, and claimable market payouts: the same thesis as Tesseract, extended until it stopped being a primitive and became a product."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "tesseract-and-zk",
      "index": "04",
      "title": "Privacy as Primitive.",
      "subtitle": "Building temporal decorrelation on Stellar.",
      "era": "S01 · 2025 : 2026",
      "date": "MAY 2025",
      "category": "Cryptography",
      "excerpt": "Deposit, wait, withdraw. A Distributor severs the link between depositors and recipients. The most boring path is the most private one.",
      "aspect": "aspect-square",
      "accent": "secondary",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Privacy as a feature is marketing. Privacy as a primitive is architecture. Tesseract makes that distinction concrete: a mechanism where a detached Distributor handles outflow, breaking the on-chain link between depositor and recipient. Channel-account rotation, hybrid encryption, and BullMQ-backed scheduling ensure that the system remains stable and private."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 05 / Mastery"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "The silent protocol."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "I came out of the Soroban season with a much narrower set of opinions and a much wider set of scars. Both compound. The result is a protocol that does one thing perfectly: it clears private payments without the theater of 'zero-knowledge' marketing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "open-assets-evolution",
      "index": "05",
      "title": "RealFi Mastery.",
      "subtitle": "Bridging the RWA liquidity gap.",
      "era": "S01 · 2025 : 2026",
      "date": "OCT 2025",
      "category": "Architecture",
      "excerpt": "From the U2U Vietbuild win to Mantle-native RWA layers. Moving beyond hackathon products to fundamental financial primitives.",
      "aspect": "aspect-square",
      "accent": "primary",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "OpenAssets found its niche in the gap between trade receivables and on-chain liquidity. We first proved the concept at the Vietbuild hackathon for U2U network, taking home the win. But a hackathon product is just a sketch. We redesigned and revamped the entire stack for the Mantle Network, focusing on ERC-3643 (T-REX) compliance and institutional-grade security."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 04 / Primitives"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "The RWA Primitive."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quote",
          "text": "If your protocol cannot handle compliant Real-World Assets with the precision of a legacy bank, you are playing a game. We built OpenAssets to be the fundamental service."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "We implemented mETH-powered leverage vaults and universal credit abstraction (OAID). It was a season of high-stakes architectural decisions, transforming a 48-hour win into a cornerstone of the Mantle ecosystem."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "payzoll-journey",
      "index": "06",
      "title": "The Mainnet Season.",
      "subtitle": "Scaling from incubation to Stellar mainnet.",
      "era": "S01 · 2024 : 2026",
      "date": "MAR 2024",
      "category": "Inflection",
      "excerpt": "PayZoll evolved from an Open Campus incubation into a global rails provider. Institutional capital moving through systems that don't sleep.",
      "aspect": "aspect-video",
      "accent": "secondary",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "PayZoll began as a thesis on friction. Capital moves slowly between borders and blockchains. Starting with the Educhain Open Campus incubation, we built for scale. The $100K SCF grant from the Stellar Development Foundation turned the prototype into a company. We focused on the vision of capital that moves now, not later."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 03 / Conviction"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "Engineering as a thesis."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "Today, we are integrating with Mudrex (Saber) to harden the liquidity bridge. We chose Rust and Go because they are languages of operators, not narratives. Scaling to Stellar mainnet isn't just a milestone, it's the result of building infrastructure that actually clears at 3am on a Sunday."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "hackathon-arc",
      "index": "07",
      "title": "The Crucible.",
      "subtitle": "4-day sprints and solo marathons.",
      "era": "S01 · 2023 : 2024",
      "date": "FEB 2024",
      "category": "Velocity",
      "excerpt": "A series of high-stakes wins across Stellar, Polkadot, and Mantle. From a 4-day solo marathon mastering path payments to winning honorary awards in new ecosystems, this is where technical intuition was forged.",
      "aspect": "aspect-square",
      "accent": "primary",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The arc reached its peak during the 4-day Stellar 'Build Better' Hackathon. Entering as a newcomer, I chose a high-risk path: solo-coding the entire infrastructure. I spent the first 12 hours deep in the core protocol docs, mapping out a solution others deemed impossible for a sprint: a complex multi-hop path payment system that handled asset conversion mid-flow. It secured 1st place and validated a core thesis: deep technical empathy is the ultimate force multiplier."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 02 / The Operator"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "Systems that clear."
        },
        {
          "kind": "quote",
          "text": "Winning is a byproduct of planning. From solo marathons on Stellar to hybrid EVM / Wasm logic on Polkadot, the goal was never just to 'finish', it was to build systems that clear under pressure."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The momentum shifted to Polkadot, where I built Kredio. As a newcomer to Substrate, I implemented cross-chain credit scoring via XCM, earning an Honorary Award. This continued with the Mantle Global Hackathon, where my team and I architected OpenAssets. These weren't trophies, they were the evidence of the production-grade architect I am today."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slug": "college-foundations",
      "index": "08",
      "title": "The Apprenticeship.",
      "subtitle": "Theory meets the wire at IIIT.",
      "era": "S01 · 2022 : 2023",
      "date": "NOV 2022",
      "category": "Origins",
      "excerpt": "IIIT Guwahati, first semester. A 9.55 CGPA and a stubborn refusal to write throwaway code. Algorithms by day, debugging Linux stacks at 2am.",
      "aspect": "aspect-[3/4]",
      "accent": "secondary",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "College began as a race for technical depth. While the syllabus covered the basics, I was building my own detention center for bugs. Writing malloc from scratch to understand segfaults, scraping portals before there was a polite reason to, and obsessing over the distance between a textbook example and the actual wire."
        },
        {
          "kind": "phase",
          "text": "Phase 01 / The Foundation"
        },
        {
          "kind": "h2",
          "text": "Learning to see the system."
        },
        {
          "kind": "p",
          "text": "The 9.55 CGPA wasn't about the grades, it was about the discipline of completion. I learned early that taste comes from quantity. The path from first-year to mainnet isn't a leap, it is a thousand boring repetitions until you stop being surprised by your own mistakes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
